<title>UK: Britain presents new scheme to EU on mad cow ban.</title>
<headline>Britain presents new scheme to EU on mad cow ban.</headline>
<dateline>LONDON 1997-02-25</dateline>
<text>
<p>British Agriculture Minister Douglas Hogg will write on Tuesday to European Union Farm Commissioner Franz Fischler giving him details of British plans for a certified cattle herd scheme, seen as a key element towards the lifting of the ban on British beef exports.</p>
<p>Government sources said the submission addressed 'a very important point' in the Florence Agreement of June 1996 which set out the steps Britain must take to get the ban lifted.  </p>
<p>The sources said Hogg will give further details of Britain's plan in a written answer to a parliamentary question at or soon after 1530 gmt.</p>
<p>The new scheme would be considered by an EU veterinary committee, and then by farm ministers, who have previously been reluctant to sanction British exports in the light of evidence that mad cow disease could be transmitted to humans.</p>
</text>
